\\text{Time} \\]\nOn the way to the park, the rate is \\( x^2 \\) miles per hour and the time is 3 hours, so the distance is:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = x^2 \\times 3 = 3x^2 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the rate and time for the return trip. The rate is \\( 16 - 4x \\) miles per hour and the time is 4 hours, so the distance is:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = (16 - 4x) \\times 4 = 4(16 - 4x) = 64 - 16x \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nSince the distance to the park and the distance back home are the same, we can set the two expressions for distance equal to each other:\n\\[ 3x^2 = 64 - 16x \\]\n\nRearrange the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\\[ 3x^2 + 16x - 64 = 0 \\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 3 \\), \\( b = 16 \\), and \\( c = -64 \\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[ x = \\frac{-16 \\pm \\sqrt{16^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (-64)}}{2 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{-16 \\pm \\sqrt{256 + 768}}{6} = \\frac{-16 \\pm \\sqrt{1024}}{6} = \\frac{-16 \\pm 32}{6} \\]\n\nThis gives us two potential solutions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{-16 + 32}{6} = \\frac{16}{6} = \\frac{8}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{-16 - 32}{6} = \\frac{-48}{6} = -8 \\]\n\nSince \\( x > 0 \\), we discard \\( x = -8 \\) and keep \\( x = \\frac{8}{3} \\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{8}{3}} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{8}{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:552"}
